# Copy to .env and fill in. The agent reads this file at startup — it looks for
# it at the root of AGENT_STATE, which is the working directory unless the
# environment says otherwise. A variable already set in the environment
# overrides the value here. Keep .env mode 600; it is gitignored.
#
# This is every setting the agent reads, and a test checks that in both
# directions: one missing from here is one nobody can find, and one listed here
# that nothing reads is worse, because changing it appears to do something.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Serving
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Not 8080: tools commonly use that port, and no tool may reach this one.
# AGENT_ADDR=:7770

# There is no setting for the model. A new conversation starts on the one last
# chosen in the interface, which the agent keeps in AGENT_STATE/data.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where things are. Two roots, and everything is derived from them.
#
#   AGENT_STATE/data        transcripts, one directory per session, and db/
#   AGENT_STATE/workspace   one working directory per session
#   AGENT_STATE/.env        this file
#
#   AGENT_HOME/tools        scanned at startup and on reload_tools
#   AGENT_HOME/skills       likewise
#   AGENT_HOME/web          the browser interface
#   AGENT_HOME/CHANGELOG.md what changed, shown under More -> version
#
# State outlives the container and is the one directory a deployment mounts.
# Home is what the image ships and never changes. The image sets them to
# /app/state and /app; from a checkout both are the working directory.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# AGENT_STATE=.
# AGENT_HOME=.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sandbox
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A tool reads and writes its own session's working directory and nothing else.
# Name any extra directory it may READ here, separated by ':'. It only adds —
# there is no setting that turns the boundary off. Nothing needs this today; it
# is the documented repair for a tool that turns out to need a path nobody
# anticipated, which otherwise fails in a way that reads as a broken tool.
# AGENT_READ_PATHS=/opt/something

# A tool may open TCP connections only to the ports software commonly talks on:
# 22, 53, 80, 443, 3000, 3306, 4000, 5000, 5173, 5432, 6379, 8000, 8080, 8443,
# 8888, 9418, 27017 — and to the agent's own tool API. Name any other port it
# may connect to here, separated by commas or spaces. It only adds, and the
# port in AGENT_ADDR is never added: a tool that could reach the operator's API
# could create a conversation holding a credential. The Tools screen shows what
# is in force.
# AGENT_TOOL_PORTS=2222,9000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials the agent holds for a tool
#
# Not granted per conversation: a tool whose manifest names one of these is
# given it on every call, in every session, and no other tool receives it. The
# Tools screen names them on that tool's card — the name, never the value.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Without it web_search cannot search, and says so. Tavily gives 1,000 searches
# a month on a free account with no card; the agent asks for one search at a
# time and no more results than it will read.
# TAVILY_API_KEY=tvly-...

# Where those searches go. There is no reason to set this: it exists so the
# test suite can put a server of its own in the way instead of the public API.
# TAVILY_URL=https://api.tavily.com/search

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials a session can be granted
#
# Not settings the agent acts on. They sit here so the values exist where the
# agent runs; a tool receives one only in a session granted it by name, under
# Controls -> granted environment. Setting them here alone changes nothing,
# which is deliberate.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Clone URL of this repository, for the agent to propose changes to itself.
# AGENT_REPO=https://x-access-token:<token>@github.com/<owner>/agent.git

# Fine-grained, one repository, contents + pull requests write, no workflow
# scope. The most it can do with this is open a pull request nobody has merged.
# GH_TOKEN=github_pat_...

# The same for GitLab, read by glab: a personal or project access token scoped
# to the projects the agent works on, api and write_repository. git takes it in
# the clone URL, https://oauth2:<token>@gitlab.com/<group>/<project>.git.
# GITLAB_TOKEN=glpat-...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not settings: AGENT_URL, AGENT_SESSION, AGENT_JOB, AGENT_DB, AGENT_DB_PREFIX,
# TMPDIR and HOME are set by the agent for each tool subprocess, and overwritten
# on every call — HOME among them, because the one this user has is not a
# directory any tool may write. The model `task eval` uses is a flag on that command, not a
# setting: `go run ./cmd/eval -model <id>`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
